00:04:59.115 --> 00:05:00.490
ALAN FREGTMAN: So
my name's Alan.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.150
I'm a pipeline
developer at Rodeo FX.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.250
The company has been
around for about 10 years,

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.810
and I've been there for four.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:10.140
And it's grown a lot
since I've been there.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:11.620
I initially started
as what's known

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.480
as a rigger, which
is just the guy who

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.630
articulates the characters
or vehicles or such.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.380
And slowly, in the
last couple of years,

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.360
I've been doing more
of a developer role,

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.990
doing more coding,
mostly Python.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.370
And it's really exciting.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.370
The studio has grown
from like 50 people

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.460
to like 300 and something
in a couple of years.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:35.630
And it's crazy.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.970
MARK MANDEL: So I can
get a bit of an inkling

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.170
from the name of the company,
but what exactly does it do?

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.550
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah, so we
do visual effects for film

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.010
and high-end TV series.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.040
We've done some work for
the last three seasons

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.210
of "Game of Thrones" and
the occasional commercial

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.020
but mostly film.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:56.520
We did some work on
the last "Star Trek,"

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.380
some work on "Deadpool,"
"Birdman," the last "Tarzan,"

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.280
"Fantastic Beasts," which
is about to come out soon,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.720
lots of creatures in that
one, really exciting,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.800
and some old stuff, like
"Pacific Rim" and "Edge

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:10.650
of Tomorrow."

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.070
MARK MANDEL: Old stuff.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:12.335
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah,

00:06:12.335 --> 00:06:13.834
MARK MANDEL: That
is pretty awesome.

00:06:13.834 --> 00:06:17.806
ALAN FREGTMAN: Well, it
seems old in my timeline.

00:06:17.806 --> 00:06:19.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No,
those are awesome.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.770
So when you described
yourself, you

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.940
used the term
"pipeline developer"?

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:25.877
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah.

00:06:25.877 --> 00:06:27.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
What is that exactly?

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.470
ALAN FREGTMAN: So a
pipeline, in my industry,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:36.750
it's basically the sort of
flow of data from one specialty

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.440
to another, so to speak.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:42.880
So there's a lot of
specialties in visual effects.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:44.700
It's really hard to
be a one-man shop.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.230
You really got to
specialize, and it

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:52.460
goes through like modeling,
texturing, shading, rigging,

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:59.250
animation, lighting and layout,
and eventually compositing.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:03.650
And to get everything
to be versioned

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.320
and to flow and to
import and export

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:10.010
and all the statuses of
everything and everything

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:15.660
to be up-to-date and correct
and valid and follow convention,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.180
there's this term
of the pipeline,

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.130
which is basically sort of
the conglomeration of tools.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:27.740
It's kind of like, if the tools
were neurons in the brain,

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.470
that the mind would
be the pipeline.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:33.850
It's sort of like the abstract,
all-encompassing concept.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.310
And a pipeline
developer basically

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.864
works towards making the flow
of the information smooth.

00:07:37.864 --> 00:07:38.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.700
So could you tell us a little
bit about what kind of tools

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:46.250
or what kind of products you
use to make these happen?

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.490
ALAN FREGTMAN: Specifically
in Google or in general?

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, in general,
like what kind of tools?

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.780
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, what
does the process look like?

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.060
Like if somebody comes to
you, and they're like, hey,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.430
we want this scene
shot in a movie.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.270
How does that look?

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:58.770
ALAN FREGTMAN: So
initially, there's

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:03.750
a bidding process where
studios say how much they will

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.120
cost to do a particular shot.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:06.680
Then it's up to the
client to decide

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.960
who to give what to
based on their reputation

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.670
and their funding, I suppose.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:17.300
Once we get a shot
and it's confirmed,

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.570
we usually receive the plates.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:23.230
Those have to be-- they
have to go through what's

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:29.190
known as match moving,
which is where some very

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:32.679
careful people track
the motion of the camera

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:34.690
with some specialized software.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.059
That is not entirely
automatic, unfortunately,

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.571
and there's a lot of manual
work to get that going.

00:08:40.571 --> 00:08:42.070
And once we have
the camera, then we

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:44.390
can start creating
creatures and vehicles

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.990
and whatever kind of
replacement or enhancement

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:48.550
that the shot requires.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.760
And those have to go through
all the various stages,

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.880
initially with modeling,
obviously concept art.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.960
If there's no concept,
there's nothing to model.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.000
But initially
modeling, then it needs

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.130
to be textured at some point.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.500
That can usually go in parallel.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.450
And eventually, that goes
into the hands of riggers,

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.590
which is what I used to be.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:14.090
And those guys, they
articulate and add

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:17.620
controls for the animators to
use to bring stuff to life,

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:24.964
be it a creature, or a prop, or
an awesome robot, or whatever.

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:26.380
And then at the
same time, there's

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:30.385
also occasional enhancements
by-- well, I say occasional.

00:09:30.385 --> 00:09:31.960
It's not really.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:35.250
There's a lot of enhancements
of simulations that happen.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:39.070
Either water
splashes, explosions,

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.351
rocks falling,
all kinds of stuff

00:09:41.351 --> 00:09:43.850
that the effects department
does in parallel, while everyone

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.880
else is trying to
do the creatures,

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.830
the vehicles, or props.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.320
And then eventually,
it all comes together

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.390
with the help of the
lighters and the render farm,

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:59.070
or hybrid render farm,
part Cloud, part local.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.100
And it goes into the hands
of the compositors, who

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.300
then composite the
final layers together

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:06.310
and make the beautiful,
consistent look

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:07.779
throughout the whole thing.

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:09.570
And then that gets
delivered to the client,

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:13.295
and they add music
and do the edit.

00:10:13.295 --> 00:10:14.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:17.990
So out of my
experience, any-- I've

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:21.450
done some rendering
of videos, and there's

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:22.540
two problems with that.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:26.860
It takes forever, and you
end up with huge files.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:29.960
How do you make it happen?

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:33.930
I'm imagining that
to make a dragon look

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:39.770
real, that's a lot of gigabytes
and a lot of processing.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.820
How do you make these
actually happen?

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.950
ALAN FREGTMAN:
Yeah, well, we have

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.350
a lot of storage on premise.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:51.090
We have a mix of
different filers

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.332
we have acquired
through the years,

00:10:53.332 --> 00:10:58.010
and it's all presented
through a caching appliance

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:02.570
that we have as one common
place for the mounts.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.790
And it's many
hundreds of terabytes.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.451
It's really intense.

00:11:07.451 --> 00:11:09.450
And some of the stuff
that we do in the pipeline

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.310
to try to minimize
the duplication

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.990
is to do symlinks when stuff
gets versioned but doesn't

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:18.630
really change.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:22.330
For example, when the textures
get versioned again and only

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:26.794
like one bit of somewhere
on the thing changed,

00:11:26.794 --> 00:11:28.210
then we try to
symlink so we don't

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:29.293
have to copy so much data.

00:11:29.293 --> 00:11:31.890
And we try to keep it light.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.370
Obviously, not always the
case, but we do what we can.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:40.680
And, of course, we use the Cloud
to be able to render really

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:41.945
massive, massive things.

00:11:41.945 --> 00:11:43.570
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
you're saying-- OK,

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:47.154
you mentioned that you've got a
lot of storage and CPU on prem.

00:11:47.154 --> 00:11:49.570
So where does the Cloud-- where
does Google Cloud Platform

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:50.820
come in?

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:54.980
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah, so we
do a lot of different movies

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:58.120
at the same time, a lot of
different shows, we call them.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:03.700
And it could be anything from
like four to a dozen shows

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.870
in different stages
of production.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:09.440
And from time to time, usually
at the end of the week,

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.790
there will be some kind
of delivery to the client.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.690
Because you deliver things as
you start to complete them,

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.800
or at least you deliver
semifinished versions

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.690
that look better and better.

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.590
And it's very important that
those deadlines must never,

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.930
ever, ever be late because
they are set in stone,

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.020
and you must deliver.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:36.440
And so my wife's boss saw the
storm coming, so to speak,

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:43.000
and he knew that, maybe a year
and a half ago, two years ago,

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.290
he saw it coming that we
would get to the point

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.400
where we just didn't
have enough hardware

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.360
to make everyone happy.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.620
And it would come that we would
have a Friday where we could

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.010
not actually deliver
what we had to deliver,

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:57.830
and we would just
disappoint the client

00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:59.590
and be in a bad position.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:03.470
And so he asked me,
a long time ago,

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:05.840
to work on a sort of
implementation that

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.590
would work together with
our current render farm

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:13.520
software in such a
way that is relatively

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.440
seamless for the artist,
because it just shows up

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:17.670
as another render note.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:21.370
It's not its own separate thing.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:24.280
It's not a whole other
completely isolated, brand

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:26.130
new system, per se.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:29.930
It sort of comes as
part of the same network

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:33.210
as the current
farm, so it almost

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.885
gets treated as if it was
a neighbor, so to speak.

00:13:35.885 --> 00:13:37.010
MARK MANDEL: And then what?

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.480
You're able to use that to
expand your capabilities

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:45.130
and sort of use that elastic
Cloud to grow as you need it?

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.050
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah.

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:50.810
Once we had everything
configured more or less right,

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:56.100
you can very easily-- we were
using the managed instance

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.850
groups feature, and we
have a few common templates

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.140
for different specs,
and usually we

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:05.050
do different specs
for different shows

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:07.760
depending on how
much they require.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:14.300
And we've been really-- we use a
lot of the dynamic-- the custom

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.430
instance, where you can drag
and drop the number of cores,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.164
the number of amount of RAM.

00:14:19.164 --> 00:14:20.080
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:23.310
ALAN FREGTMAN: Ever
since that was an option,

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:24.670
we've been using it a lot.

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:26.180
And we always use
custom instances

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.596
because sometimes you don't
really need all that much RAM,

00:14:28.596 --> 00:14:29.730
but we need more cores.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:32.820
And I'm excited to
say that the Cloud

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.490
machines, that the top
of the Cloud machines,

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:40.260
are considerably better than
what we have on premise.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.240
And it's an ongoing race.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.680
I mean, the second
that we-- it's

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:48.220
very expensive to do a
massive upgrade of whatever,

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:51.150
200 notes that we have
locally, and get them

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:52.810
all to the latest specs.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:55.740
And then within a few months,
there's something new.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:00.860
And having the Cloud, being
able to burst so easily,

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:03.920
in a matter of seconds you can
launch hundreds of machines,

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:08.630
and it's so fast
and so effortless

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:12.480
that it's a great thing to
have just when you really

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.080
need to burst and
you need resources

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:19.580
to finish the computation
of producing those images.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:23.310
We use a renderer called
Arnold, which was recently

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.260
acquired by Autodesk,
and we do most

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:32.240
of our work out of this
Maya and effects in Houdini,

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.750
and compositing in Nuke

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:34.666
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:34.666 --> 00:15:38.100
So as soon as you started
talking about rendering,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.680
preemptible machines
came to mind.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:45.239
Is this something that you use,
or have you consider using it?

00:15:45.239 --> 00:15:46.030
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yes.

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:48.030
We actually do use them.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:52.760
We don't use them exclusively,
because at the moment,

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:54.760
it's up to the discretion
of the wrangler, which

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:59.430
is the person-- the human
being managing the farm

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:01.260
or overlooking the farm.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:02.770
It's up to their
discretion how many

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:04.910
machines should
come up, depending

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.540
on what our resources are.

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:09.104
Like we only have so many.

00:16:09.104 --> 00:16:10.520
We have a fair
number of licenses,

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:12.390
but we only have so many.

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:16.150
So sometimes we'll need a bit
more on local, then a bit more

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:16.950
on Cloud.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:18.010
It all depends.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.620
It fluctuates a lot,
and it's a hard task

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:23.850
to do programmatically.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.590
So for now, we need
the human element

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.810
of a wrangler to make the call.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:33.290
Now, that said, we
do have scripts.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:35.820
Once the machines come
on and they become idle,

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:39.480
they start to die very quickly.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.710
One of the great things
with the Google Cloud

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.282
is the per-minute billing.

00:16:44.282 --> 00:16:45.740
I know there's a
10-minute minimum,

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:49.020
but after that, it's
per minute, and we

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:51.710
have a script that, after
the second idle minute,

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:52.620
the machine dies.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:57.470
So we really try not to
waste any of the resources.

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:01.510
But to start them, at one
point we had given the artists

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:03.614
the ability to start
their own machines,

00:17:03.614 --> 00:17:04.780
and they got a little crazy.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:09.829
They would start a few
too many, and we quickly

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:13.854
realized that it had to
be overlooked by someone.

00:17:13.854 --> 00:17:14.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.890
So I'm imagining
that at any point,

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:21.109
you have a decent amount of--
decent number of machines

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:21.980
running.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:26.010
How do you make sure that they
all have a good configuration?

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.380
How do you manage
the software install

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:31.027
licenses and all that stuff?

00:17:31.027 --> 00:17:32.610
ALAN FREGTMAN: So
just before-- I just

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:34.490
remembered one thing
about the preemptibles.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:35.570
We do use them.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:38.350
The thing is that we don't
use them exclusively,

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:44.030
because the way that they work
is they will die eventually

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:46.050
without notice.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.680
But while the wrangler
is physically present

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.070
and at work, we will try to
use them as much as we can,

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:56.480
and then when they leave for
the night, we will do a switch,

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:58.966
and we'll start
some regular VMs.

00:17:58.966 --> 00:18:00.690
And that way, we
know that there's

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:03.557
going to be some VMs left,
just to be totally sure.

00:18:03.557 --> 00:18:05.140
MARK MANDEL: Is that
so if they happen

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:06.848
to shut down while
the person is looking,

00:18:06.848 --> 00:18:09.290
they can manually restart them?

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.210
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah,
basically that,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.780
because they're more
likely to survive.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.000
But during the day,
we use them as much

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.510
as we can when we use the Cloud.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:25.560
And so to go to the next
question that you just asked,

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.526
we coordinate all the
configuration with SaltStack.

00:18:28.526 --> 00:18:31.070
I don't know if you're
familiar with the technology.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:31.655
MARK MANDEL: Can
you explain-- yeah,

00:18:31.655 --> 00:18:33.610
explain for our
listeners, so for people

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.390
who aren't as familiar with it.

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:38.360
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah, it's a
configuration and deployment

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:40.400
tool.

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.170
The core is written in Python,
and I like Python so I like it.

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.400
It's quite configurable and
really, really flexible.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.310
And it is very straightforward.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:55.280
I mean, there's other
things like Ansible or Chef.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:56.120
They're similar.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:57.870
I haven't tried
many of the others,

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.610
but I really like Salt.
It's very easy to configure.

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:04.780
It's all based on YAML files,
which are very human readable.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:11.240
You can have conditional
templating and multiple masters

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.320
if you want.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.550
It's quite flexible.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:19.560
And so we actually
use, for example--

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:24.130
we query some of the VM at
the data on the machine,

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:26.750
to configure itself.

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:31.480
So we set some tags and some--
well, not environment variable,

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.240
some metadata variables.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.830
And we use those
to, for example,

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:39.910
configure which show that
the slave will be for.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:43.170
And that's using Salt to read
the metadata to then configure

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.920
the machine on first boot.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.420
MARK MANDEL: So like you're
talking about the person who's

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.930
working on the show, they
can fire up machines,

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:53.910
and they can then specify like
hey, I need these resources,

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:56.301
and then Salt will go off
and configure those resources

00:19:56.301 --> 00:19:56.800
for them.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:57.980
Does that sound about right?

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:00.396
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah, basically
we use the managed instance

00:20:00.396 --> 00:20:02.730
groups with the custom
templates that we

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.380
keep cloning a-- sort
of the default template.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:09.680
We change a few attributes
for the specs and what show.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.750
We have a code name for
each show, what code

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:15.690
name it will be for, and then
those machines will launch.

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:18.150
Given whatever number
you request of them,

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:21.180
they will appear,
and they will begin

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:24.840
taking jobs from the shows.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:25.670
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:28.895
So you've been talking about
how you have an on prem and then

00:20:28.895 --> 00:20:30.870
a Cloud shared solution.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:33.930
How are you like-- I'm guessing
there are some assets that will

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.977
need to go like up and down.

00:20:35.977 --> 00:20:36.810
How is that managed?

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:38.601
What sort of challenges
have you got there?

00:20:38.601 --> 00:20:41.550
ALAN FREGTMAN:
Yeah, so initially

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:47.520
we tried stuff like
rsync and maybe even SCP,

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.520
which is not fast.

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:51.330
And it's very single threaded.

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:54.630
It works, but it's
really not fast.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:56.220
And then I started
looking at options,

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:00.990
and there's a few that
are sort of flood via UDP

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:03.330
to maximize the throughput.

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:05.550
But the one that really
took the cake for me

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:09.450
was a piece of technology
from the guys at CERN,

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:12.910
the supercollider people.

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:16.240
They have a software called FDT.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.580
It's very originally
called Fast Data Transfer,

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:22.640
and it's made in Java.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:23.890
They don't provide the source.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:28.170
It's not technically open
source, but it is free to use.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:31.240
And it's incredibly fast.

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:36.030
You can use it over
a VPN SSH channel,

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:38.210
or directly if you want.

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:42.120
And basically it's
a bit like Aspera,

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:44.027
if you know what that is.

00:21:44.027 --> 00:21:45.610
A lot of people use
it in my industry,

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:48.510
but Aspera is very expensive.

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:50.360
FDT is the same idea.

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:55.220
It's a a flood of UDP packets
that maximize your pipe,

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:59.380
and it's extremely concurrent.

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:03.160
You can start hundreds of
threads with a bunch of data,

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.670
and it all arrives
at the same time.

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:08.450
And it's so far the
only tool that we've

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:13.490
seen that can really
absolutely maximize our gigabit

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:16.404
pipe that we have
going to the internet.

00:22:16.404 --> 00:22:17.820
MARK MANDEL: Are
you able to share

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:19.778
how fast you're able to
get some data transfers

00:22:19.778 --> 00:22:22.220
or things you may have seen?

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.855
ALAN FREGTMAN: Well, we
only have a gigabit internet

00:22:24.855 --> 00:22:28.450
at our studio at the moment,
and we pretty much fill it up.

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:32.220
We had like 800 to 900 megabits.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:35.790
And we had a few times
when I was getting

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:38.640
started with this technology
that I accidentally

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:42.100
choked the network,
and for a few seconds

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:45.600
we lost internet because
it was too fast, which

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.670
I guess is a good problem to
have, better than too slow.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:50.010
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.910
Now I'm also curious,
I guess you probably

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:55.950
work on some relatively big
budget and somewhat secret type

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:57.260
projects.

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:59.410
What do you have in
terms of security?

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:00.910
What sort of
considerations have you

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:02.935
had that way to make
sure everything is not

00:23:02.935 --> 00:23:04.310
public to the
internet, probably,

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.620
in any way, shape, or form?

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:07.570
ALAN FREGTMAN: Yeah,
we try to-- well,

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:12.340
we use the Google
Cloud Platform firewall

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:16.480
to pretty much block all access,
except to the IP from Montreal.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:19.720
That's one thing, and we try
to use VPN wherever possible.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:24.750
And we believe that's
reasonably secure.

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:26.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how are you using VPN?

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:29.650
Is that so the machines
that are on the Cloud

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:32.330
are able to access
the on-prem storage,

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:35.230
or do you use it for
some other things?

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:38.610
ALAN FREGTMAN: We
use it for licensing,

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:42.230
for licensing and to actually
talk to the farm software.

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:45.210
We use a software called Qube!

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.360
With a Q. It's from a
company called PipelineFX.

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:52.592
It's quite flexible.

00:23:52.592 --> 00:23:54.050
We have used it
for many years now,

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:56.140
and we have customized it a lot.

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.119
And it's not rocket science.

00:23:58.119 --> 00:23:59.410
It's basically a queue manager.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:06.150
And people make jobs.

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:07.780
They give them attributes.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.950
They give them what
cluster they should go for,

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.510
how much RAM they
should have, and so on.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:16.430
And the machines
appear just the same

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:19.700
as the machines on the
local machines up here.

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:22.300
The Cloud ones are
just in the same place,

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.120
in the same interface,
the same everything.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:26.000
So it's quite seamless.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:27.860
The only thing is
that we require

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:31.020
having an upload
of the things that

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:35.290
are required by your scene file,
which is like one extra job.

00:24:35.290 --> 00:24:37.900
And we require one
extra download job

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:39.630
so that the files,
as they complete,

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:41.320
they get downloaded back.

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:44.391
And we use FDT for that,
and it's very, very fast.

00:24:44.391 --> 00:24:46.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What is the
process for someone to be like,

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:50.080
oh, we have this scene that
we need to process right now,

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:51.130
very fast.

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:53.369
Do you have a turbo
button or something?

00:24:53.369 --> 00:24:54.410
ALAN FREGTMAN: Oh, I see.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: To start like a
bunch of instances or something

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:57.200
like that?

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:59.075
ALAN FREGTMAN: We do
not have a turbo button,

00:24:59.075 --> 00:25:02.180
but we do, basically-- when
you submit a job to the Cloud,

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:03.930
it's the same as
submitting a job locally.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.280
There's just a check box
that says use the Cloud.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:08.490
And it just configures
the job differently.

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:11.260
It scans for the
dependencies of the thing

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:12.275
that you're rendering.

00:25:12.275 --> 00:25:14.710
It tries to find all the
terabytes worth of textures,

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:18.830
and models, and
cameras, and animations,

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:21.900
and puts it in a big
upload job through FDT,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:24.410
and then it submits to
the farm, just as it

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:27.354
would be without the Cloud.

00:25:27.354 --> 00:25:28.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:30.590
So we're kind of
running out of time.

00:25:30.590 --> 00:25:32.420
But is there anything
else that you'd

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:36.760
like to mention or something
that we had forgotten?

00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:38.570
ALAN FREGTMAN: So
an interesting thing

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:40.320
I guess I'd like to
mention, is that we're

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:43.470
using Lustre FS for storage
solution in the Cloud.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:46.710
It's an open source project
that allows very, very

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:50.440
easy distributed
redundant storage

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:52.240
across multiple machines.

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:54.320
In this case, we use VMs.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:26:00.540
And it's a way to get
faster reads than just one

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.370
machine, one disk.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.880
And it allows us to
scale really easily.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:07.686
It's very easy to
add more-- they

00:26:07.686 --> 00:26:10.280
call it bricks, more
sort of units of storage.

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:13.250
It's sort kind of like
adding more disks.

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:17.600
And it makes it very
easy and very fast,

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:19.690
and it's open source.

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:21.960
It takes like 10
minutes to get started

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.060
to do your first shared volume.

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.990
It's really quite wonderful.

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:30.020
And I had tried--
the very first time

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:32.550
that I started in the
Cloud, I tried to see

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.230
how fast could I get just
one machine with like an NFS

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:39.310
share that would be just
shared to a few machines.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:44.199
And after something like 40
machines, it was just too much.

00:26:44.199 --> 00:26:45.240
It just couldn't take it.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.990
And that wasn't Google's
fault. It's just NFS is really

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:49.210
not the way to go.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:52.850
One node cannot store
all your storage.

00:26:52.850 --> 00:26:55.120
That's not going to scale.

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:59.910
But at the moment, we have
like six storage nodes,

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:03.040
and they're all sharing
several terabytes worth,

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:04.530
and it's very fast.

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:06.330
It's comparable to
what we have locally.

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:07.746
MARK MANDEL: Out
of curiosity, you

00:27:07.746 --> 00:27:10.522
didn't use Google Cloud storage
because Lustre FS gives you

00:27:10.522 --> 00:27:11.980
some other capability
or something?

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.032
Or what's the deal there?

00:27:14.032 --> 00:27:16.490
ALAN FREGTMAN: Well, we didn't
use-- there's an open source

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:18.940
project to mount the
Google Cloud storage,

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:23.180
but it's not super
stable or super tested,

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:27.560
so I did not trust it
for serious production.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:30.210
And Lustre just
allows us to have

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:35.080
a safe, redundant, shared
volume that something like NFS

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:36.460
would not be able to give us.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:40.510
And we can't directly--
without mounting--

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:42.925
without having something to
mount a Google Cloud storage,

00:27:42.925 --> 00:27:44.540
we can't directly use it.

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:47.230
So we use it as sort of
a transitionary place

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.750
or as a place for backups
and stuff like that.

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:52.160
MARK MANDEL: That's fine, cool.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.914
Well, Alan, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:27:54.914 --> 00:27:56.080
That was really interesting.

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:56.920
ALAN FREGTMAN: Thank you.

00:27:56.920 --> 00:27:58.669
MARK MANDEL: The world
