00:28:25.101 --> 00:28:25.600
Awesome.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:29.230
So why don't we get stuck into
the question of the week, which

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:31.070
comes from the wonderful
community members

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:35.580
from the Google Cloud
Platform Slack Channel.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:38.710
We were talking about-- on
there the other day about

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:42.220
say you have a Google Compute
Engine virtual machine,

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:43.850
and since you have
it up and running,

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:45.266
and you think to
yourself, oh, I'd

00:28:45.266 --> 00:28:46.774
love to be able to scale this.

00:28:46.774 --> 00:28:48.190
So what I want to
be able to do is

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:51.050
create an instance group, which
lets me scale that up and down

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:52.490
real easily.

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:55.390
Is there a way that I can
create a instance group

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:58.410
from an already running VM?

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:00.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I actually had

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:04.100
to do this a long time ago
for a demo, which is actually

00:29:04.100 --> 00:29:05.830
open source, and
we've talked about it

00:29:05.830 --> 00:29:07.510
before, it's called Abelana.

00:29:07.510 --> 00:29:11.220
And basically when I was in
the development phase of it,

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:14.940
I created an instance and then
installed ImageMagick on it.

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:17.130
And so ImageMagick was
actually a couple--

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.500
like it took me some time.

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:20.940
Then I realized that you could
do a pseudo sudo apt-get.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:21.440
That's all.

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:22.550
Whatever.

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:27.370
But it took me some time to get
the whole environment working

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:29.370
correctly, and the
next step is like,

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:32.330
oh, OK, so now I want
to have this instance

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:34.950
but just running more of them.

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:37.400
And it is actually quite simple.

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:39.430
If you are in
development phase and you

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:41.800
can delete that instance,
it is pretty simple.

00:29:41.800 --> 00:29:45.250
You can delete the instance
by making sure that you do not

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:47.990
delete the disk attached to it.

00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:52.770
And then you can create a
new image from that disk.

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:56.180
And now when you create
your instance template

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.260
in the managed
instance group, you

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:01.510
can use that new image
that you just created.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:02.620
So with that, you're good.

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:05.080
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that's
not too many steps.

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:06.621
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very simple.

00:30:06.621 --> 00:30:09.570
So from your instance with the
disk, you delete the instance,

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:12.390
because to create
a new image, you

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:14.870
need a disk that is not
attached to anything.

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:18.090
So you first delete the instance
without deleting the disk,

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:22.130
then create an image, and
then from there, the template.

00:30:22.130 --> 00:30:24.460
MARK MANDEL: So if we
have a VM that's running,

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:26.626
and you still want to be
able to work on it, though,

00:30:26.626 --> 00:30:28.980
and you don't want to delete
it, what do we do then?

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:32.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that is when
it becomes a little bit more

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:33.350
complicated.

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:34.891
Because basically
what you need to do

00:30:34.891 --> 00:30:36.660
is an extra step
to generate a disk

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:38.580
that is not attached
to anything.

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.564
And to do that,
there's a little trick

00:30:40.564 --> 00:30:41.980
that I did not
know about, but you

00:30:41.980 --> 00:30:47.420
told me, which is create
a snapshot from the disk

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:49.820
and then a disk
from the snapshot.

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:51.120
And then the rest is the same.

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:51.640
MARK MANDEL: The
rest is the same.

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:53.014
So you can create
your image, you

00:30:53.014 --> 00:30:54.690
can create your
instance template,

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:55.780
and you're good to go.

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:58.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which is--
it is actually pretty simple.

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:01.470
And that way, you are
able to go basically

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:04.260
from your prototype to
something that actually scales,

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:07.550
and you can do whatever
you want from it,

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:11.330
which is the normal
development process that I use.

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:14.150
The next step is normally,
oh, so now that everything

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:16.320
